	<body>
		<p class="cs2E86D3A6"><span class="cs5925EB86">HR 9 &nbsp;- AS INTRODUCED</span></p><p class="cs2E86D3A6"><span class="cs5925EB86">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs2E86D3A6"><span class="csDD5E5F52">2025 SESSION</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	25-0470</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	02/11</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs80D9435B" style="tab-stops:left 86.45pt left 180pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">HOUSE RESOLUTION	</span><span class="csED7826F6">9</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csDA99B56C"><span class="csDD5E5F52">A RESOLUTION	</span><a name="bkTitleDraft0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">urging the department of education to emphasize STEM education in public schools.</span></a></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csDA99B56C" style="tab-stops:left 108pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">SPONSORS:	Rep. W. Thomas, Hills. 12; Rep. N. Germana, Ches. 15</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csDA99B56C" style="tab-stops:left 108pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">COMMITTEE:	Education Policy and Administration </span></p><p class="cs95E872D0" style="tab-stops:left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0" style="tab-stops:left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">-----------------------------------------------------------------</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0" style="tab-stops:left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs2E86D3A6"><span class="csDD5E5F52">ANALYSIS</span></p><p class="cs2E86D3A6"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs9224F58F" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 86.45pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	This house resolution urges the department of education to emphasize STEM education in New Hampshire public schools.</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - </span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csC970676" style="tab-stops:left 13.5pt left 81.4pt left 94.5pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csC970676" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	25-0470</span></p><p class="csC970676" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	02/11</span></p><p class="cs95E872D0"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs41ED3027" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">STATE OF NEW HAMPSHIRE</span></p><p class="cs41ED3027" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="cs41ED3027" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csE3E0FE0B">In the Year of Our Lord Two Thousand Twenty Five</span></p><p class="csC970676" style="tab-stops:left 396pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csDA99B56C"><span class="csDD5E5F52">A RESOLUTION	</span><a name="bkTitleDraft1"><span class="csDD5E5F52">urging the department of education to emphasize STEM education in public schools.</span></a></p><p class="csD15247B9"><span class="csDD5E5F52">&nbsp;</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, in New Hampshire, there is a limited number of high schools offering classes dedicated to robotics, programming, engineering, or upper-level mathematics.  These subjects are very important and should be introduced at an earlier educational stage, especially considering the recent availability of robotics engineering programs in over 100 colleges.  The current lack of attention paid to these subjects in public schools is problematic.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, the demand for majors in robotics and engineering is projected to increase significantly in the coming years.  Schools that do not offer specialized classes in these areas are missing a valuable opportunity to prepare students for a thriving job market.  With these classes, students will have the opportunity to decide what they are passionate about and what career path they want to pursue.  The growth rate for these fields is anticipated to surpass the average growth rate of approximately 4 percent.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, robotics engineering positions are experiencing significant growth, with demand projected to increase by 9 percent between 2020 and 2030, outpacing the average growth rate for all occupations.  This surge can be attributed to several factors, including the expanding use of robotic machinery across various industries such as production and medicine.  In today&rsquo;s fast-paced technological landscape, robotics serves many practical purposes through the research, design, and construction of innovative robots.  Currently, approximately 170,000 robotics engineers are employed in the United States, reflecting the field&rsquo;s rapid expansion and its critical role in our economy.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, specific robotics-related majors are anticipated to experience notable growth in the coming years.  Software engineering, for instance, has seen a remarkable increase of 24 percent between 2016 and 2024, driven by the widespread use of software in everyday appliances.  As new applications and technologies emerge, the demand for software engineers will continue to rise.  Similarly, electrical engineering is projected to grow by 9 percent between 2023 and 2033, mostly due to the increasing need for professionals who can repair electronic devices and infrastructure.  Mechanical engineering is also expected to see an 11 percent growth during the same period, as the demand for automation and the development of new products necessitate more engineers to design and maintain these innovations.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, several technology-related jobs are predicted to be in high demand by 2025.  Full-stack developers, who work on both front-end and back-end development, are expected to be crucial as organizations seek versatile talent.  Application developers will be essential for creating applications across desktop, web, and mobile platforms, while mobile developers will focus specifically on applications for mobile devices.  Additionally, systems engineers will play a vital role in designing, integrating, and managing complex systems throughout their life cycle.  Data and business analysts will also be in demand, as they collect, process, and analyze data to assist businesses in making strategic decisions.  The overall expected growth rate for web development jobs is 8 percent, driven by the continued expansion of e-commerce.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, expanding the accessibility of STEM education early on offers a head start to students already interested in these fields while also sparking interest in those who may not yet be aware of these career paths.  By introducing these subjects in high school, students can explore careers in high-growth fields&mdash;like robotics engineering, software development, and applied mathematics&mdash;while still in a supportive learning environment.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, with more STEM-focused courses, students can make informed decisions about their future careers and take foundational courses for college, giving them a competitive edge.  Additionally, more high school graduates with STEM skills will help to strengthen local and national workforces, creating a pipeline of talent ready to innovate in robotics, engineering, data analytics, and beyond.  Introducing these subjects not only addresses a gap in the current curriculum but also fosters a culture of curiosity and problem-solving essential to tomorrow&rsquo;s workforce.</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Whereas, this expansion in STEM education would enable New Hampshire schools to adapt to the evolving demands of the tech-driven economy and empower students to explore a wealth of career opportunities, creating a better-prepared, tech-savvy generation; now, therefore, be it</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	Resolved by the House of Representatives:</span></p><p class="csF0A1D375" style="tab-stops:left 18pt left 36pt left 54pt left 72pt left 90pt;"><span class="csDD5E5F52">	That the general court and by extension the department of education advocate for the installation of STEM curricula within New Hampshire public high schools. &nbsp;Such curriculums include dedicated courses in robotics, advanced programming, engineering, and higher-level mathematics. &nbsp;The purpose of this initiative shall be to develop robust classes in each of the aforementioned areas, ensuring students not only meet basic standards but gain the exposure needed to build genuine interest and expertise in the previously mentioned fields. &nbsp;Introducing specialized courses earlier in students&rsquo; academic careers would prepare them to excel in college-level STEM programs and equip them with essential skills to succeed in the evolving workforce.</span></p></body>
